WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.190
Sony Pictures Entertainment is reeling

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.400
from what may be the biggest

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.230
and most devastating computer hacking

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.870
in Hollywood's history.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:08.750
Hackers attacked in waves.

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.650
First, crippling Sony's internal systems,

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.730
then leaking five Sony movies online.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.170
Today, the U.S. government

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.160
pointed the finger of blame
directly at North Korea

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.080
for the devastating cyber
attack against Sony Pictures.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.050
Sony pulling the plug on the interview,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.940
saying it will not show
the controversial comedy

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.263
about an assassination attempt
on North Korea's leader.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.200
The Sony Pictures hacking
was taken very seriously

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.510
because it was an attack
on the free world.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.130
What we had been building,

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.810
in terms of our
civilization and our freedom

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.850
and our ability to
exchange information freely

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:50.050
had been disrupted

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.543
by a country that we
knew very little about.

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:56.960
North Korea has reportedly made attempts

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.070
to steal Covid-19 vaccine technology

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:02.317
by hacking pharmaceutical giant Pfizer.

00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:04.200
The Justice Department has charged

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.700
three North Korean hackers

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.470
over a wide-ranging scheme
that included the attempt theft

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.670
of billions of dollars
from multiple companies.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.470
Despite so many attacks
blamed on North Korea,

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.080
there was not much known

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.550
about the people behind
the hacking attacks.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.760
And my aim was to reveal
who these people are,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.160
what they are like, what
their operations are like

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.534
and on what their dreams and fears were.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.980
My name is Sam Kim.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:42.080
I'm a reporter for Bloomberg News,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.110
and I wrote a story for Businessweek

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:45.860
about North Korean hackers.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:49.120
My story is about people who
are sent from North Korea

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.570
to countries like China
to hack for the regime,

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.100
spy on enemies

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:55.993
and make money.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:03.410
North Korea's cyber abilities
began about in the 1970s

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.680
with a lot of students going to countries

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:11.680
like Russia, China and even
East Germany to learn coding.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.430
It was aimed at improving the productivity

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.150
of factories at home.

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.690
But in some time in the 1980s and 1990s,

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.900
the current leader Kim Jong
Un's father, Kim Jong Il,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.260
thought that it would be a
good idea to develop a team,

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:32.260
an army of people who could
hack for North Korea's military.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.750
So they began this team

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.420
of basically hackers,

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.510
who would spy for the North Korean regime

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.223
and also go out there and make
hard currency for the regime.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:51.800
I think Kim Jong Un takes the
hacking ability more seriously

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.180
than his father.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.860
He also enjoys a bit of
tech savviness as well.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:02.440
You know, he's often shown
traveling on his airplane,

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.360
where on the desk, there's a Mac computer.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.780
He understands the
importance of computers.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.170
He understands the importance
of employing technology

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:15.283
to improve productivity of his economy.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.200
North Korea is an isolated country

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:29.200
with its economy pretty
much dilapidated nowadays.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.660
It's been under increasing
pressure and sanctions

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:35.960
from the U.S. and the
international community basically.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:39.170
It does not have the resources and money

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.170
to build the kind of conventional weapons

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:43.940
that other countries are able to build.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:46.400
So what happens is
North Korea concentrates

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:51.080
its resources on building
nuclear arms and missiles,

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.340
which experts call basically
asymmetric weapons.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.060
It's the kind of weapons
that gives North Korea

00:03:58.060 --> 00:03:59.247
an instant edge.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.260
Think of cyber-hacking abilities

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:15.690
in that context. It doesn't
cost them much money.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.020
But if you have the right people,

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.000
and if you have the right training,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.600
you are able to cultivate an
army of people who are able

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.410
to penetrate into computer
systems of other countries.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:29.260
And that's what North Korea is after.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.140
But if you consider the
fact that there are only

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:39.080
about 1,000 IP addresses
in North Korea available,

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.020
and many of them are basically loaned

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.780
from Chinese communications companies,

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.330
you can kind of tell why
North Korea is willing to

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:52.330
send its hackers abroad rather
than operate from within.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.960
What's the matter with
operating from within?

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.320
Well,

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:00.360
first of all, it would mean
that a lot of information

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.880
would have to be coming
in from the outside world,

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.160
which could risk exposing the government

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:12.150
and the people to whatever
truth there might be about them.

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:16.940
So there they're sending
their trained hackers to China

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:21.150
and other countries where
access to information is better.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.400
But at the same time,
because these are allies,

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.230
and also because they have
their diplomat station

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.720
in countries like these, like China,

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:33.230
they're able to regulate and
control these hackers better.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:36.420
But formally, North Korea denies engaging

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.560
in hacking and describes accusations

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.523
to that effect as enemy propaganda.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:45.710
It took me at least half a year

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:47.620
to track down these people.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.930
It felt like a wild goose
chase in the beginning

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:54.490
because, see, a lot of
the people like these

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:58.523
are protected by the
government in South Korea.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.310
But at the end,

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:05.810
I was able to find a person
who had pretty extensive ties

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.330
to the North Korean-defected
community here.

00:06:08.330 --> 00:06:11.290
And then, I was also able to gain access

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:15.310
to the people who had actually
done programming hacking

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:18.380
for North Korea before
coming to South Korea.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:19.850
And Jong is one of them.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.980
Jong actually is not a real name.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:29.950
I knew before meeting Jong
that he was in his late 30s.

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:31.460
But when I actually met him,

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.280
he looked at least a decade older.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:35.230
He wouldn't open up.

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:36.530
I mean, it took me, I think

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:40.350
at least an hour to make sure
that he would trust me enough

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:43.236
that he would share
with me his life story.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:48.780
When he was first sent to China,

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:52.170
he was a student studying
computer science at a university

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:54.440
near the border with North Korea.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:58.090
And he came back and realized
that there was a lot more

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:02.130
to do in China because he had
more access to information,

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:05.360
and he asked that he'd
be sent back to China.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.470
And there was also recruitment going on

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:11.340
for people who would actually
do coding officially,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.560
but really hacking for North Korea.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.350
So, he applied and he got the job.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:19.250
Once he got to the border,
he crossed by foot.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.930
And from there, he took a bus.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:25.470
And then when he arrived at the station

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.730
that he would spend years in later,

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:30.930
he learned that that three-story building

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.430
in the heart of the city
was actually provided

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:38.250
for free actually by a Chinese man

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.683
who had business ties in North Korea.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.453
There were actually two
other people that I had met.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.970
They all said that they go
into this freelance website for

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:53.300
programmers, and clients from
around the world would come

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:56.720
and give them orders,
give them jobs to do.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.520
Some of the jobs given to
Jong were cracking open

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.040
commercially successful
antivirus programs.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.680
And once he was able to open that up,

00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:12.460
he replicated that program
into another product,

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:15.700
which his client would repackage

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:19.240
as a whole different product
to be sold to other people.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.330
He also created what we now call bots.

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:25.970
There are very popular online games,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.030
including Lineage.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:32.450
He would create a bot that
would just roam around

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:36.360
without any supervision from the user

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:41.360
and basically trade items
with other users on that game.

00:08:41.625 --> 00:08:43.565
He would grow that bot on Lineage,

00:08:43.565 --> 00:08:46.750
and sell it for almost $100.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.310
Another defector said the
money from clients was

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:53.420
wired primarily via PayPal to
accounts controlled by minders

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:55.460
because they were the
only ones with access

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.103
to legitimate bank accounts.

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.970
Jong had to make at least $100,000 a year,

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:04.700
and he doesn't get to keep most of it.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:07.950
Actually, most of the
money goes to minders,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.640
to the North Korean regime.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:11.940
But if you don't meet the quota,

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.180
the minders will make sure

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.200
that they'll put enough pressure on you.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.860
And if that happens repeatedly,

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.453
you will face the growing
odds of being sent back home.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:28.340
Among the three people that I spoke to,

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:30.240
there's a common thing
that they all tell me,

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:32.750
which is that on every Saturday,

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:34.850
they have to do this propaganda session,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:37.350
which is basically about gathering

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:38.910
all of the hackers together

00:09:38.910 --> 00:09:43.910
into a room, and they have
to recite whatever messages,

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:46.720
whatever lines come from Pyongyang,

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.780
about the Kim family, about socialism.

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:53.550
If you're especially someone
with a free set of mind,

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.240
that's stressful.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:57.920
One person actually was
really stressed out.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:00.550
What he heard about North Korea

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:03.700
once he was in China during
the propaganda sessions,

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:04.630
he's just couldn't take it.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:28.000
Jong was a senior member of
the unit that he belonged to,

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.080
and he was good at Chinese as well.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.000
Whenever the senior North
Korean official came

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:38.000
from North Korea to check on
the hacking units in China,

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:40.700
Jong would accompany that official.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:43.600
He doesn't provide me details,

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:47.880
but he calls it "unsavory
incident" that happened

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:51.733
between him and an official
that he was traveling with.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:56.620
And that altercation prompted him

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:57.693
to defect.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:02.823
But there was no one that he
could turn to after defecting.

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:07.453
He would in disguise roam around China.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.600
He told me that he actually enjoyed it.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:14.460
He actually enjoyed the
freedom of, you know,

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:16.430
not being known to anybody,

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:19.453
just roaming around a
country that was huge.

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:24.903
How did he make the money
that he needed to carry on?

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:27.800
He did the same thing he did, you know,

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:28.830
at the North Korean unit.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:30.120
He would take orders.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:33.253
He would reverse engineer
software to make money.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:37.070
But at one point, he said he
was just sick and tired of it

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:37.903
all of a sudden.

00:11:37.903 --> 00:11:39.937
It just, he wanted to find a place

00:11:39.937 --> 00:11:42.600
where he could just settle down.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:46.093
And he contacted South Korean authorities.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:49.890
Now, he lives in Korea.

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:51.410
He met a woman here.

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:54.400
You know, last time I
checked, they had a baby.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.690
He worked for a software company,

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:59.643
ironically an antivirus company.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.530
What keeps coming up in my head,

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:10.210
even long after I spoke to these people,

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:14.090
is there was one common thing about them,

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:16.700
which was the sense of disillusionment.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:20.290
Once they had access
to outside information,

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:24.693
once they were able to
freely roam in the internet,

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.653
there was a spark.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:32.130
And once they realized
that there's a discrepancy

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:34.280
between what they think
is really happening

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:37.720
and what North Korea is telling
them every Saturday morning,

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:40.423
that's when the spark gets bigger.

00:12:42.790 --> 00:12:47.790
That little fire is what
eventually drives them

00:12:47.960 --> 00:12:49.750
to decide to leave.

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:53.364
Even if it takes a huge
risk on their lives.

